{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013iMEwUGS5E3cwEa57sepav","type":"tool_result","content":"{\"result\":\"{\\\"success\\\": true, \\\"data\\\": {\\\"path\\\": \\\"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_4/projects/trace-verdict-study/memory.md\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"# Project Memory\\\\n\\\\n## westd-11235 训练命令规范 (C002)\\\\n- 必须用绝对路径 `/root/miniconda3/bin/python`（不是 `python`）\\\\n- Qwen-7B 及以上必须 `--gradient_checkpointing`\\\\n- 模型路径: `/root/trace-verdict-study/models/Qwen2.5-Coder-7B-Instruct`\\\\n- Qwen-1.5B 路径: `/root/trace-verdict-study/models/Qwen2.5-Coder-1.5B-Instruct`\\\\n- CodeLlama-13B 路径: `/root/autodl-tmp/models/CodeLlama-13b-Instruct-hf/`\\\\n- DeepSeek 路径: `/root/autodl-tmp/models/deepseek-coder-6.7b-instruct/deepseek-ai/deepseek-coder-6___7b-instruct`\\\\n- 不支持 `--epochs` 参数，脚本用 `--max_steps`（默认 -1 = 全量训练，444 steps ≈ 3 epochs）\\\\n- nohup 提交后必须注册 cron 监控（否则无回调）\\\\n- submit_training_job 自动注册 cron + 含 auto-eval chain（优先用这个）\\\\n\\\\n## Output Dir 规范\\\\n- 每个实验必须有独立 output_dir: `/root/autodl-tmp/trace-verdict-study-checkpoints/{exp_id}/`\\\\n- seed123 事故教训：未指定 seed 后缀导致覆盖 1.5B checkpoint\\\\n\\\\n## CodeLlama-13B 必须用 label_only_loss (2026-05-18)\\\\n- CodeLlama tokenizer 产生更多 token（平均 ~1174 vs Qwen ~978），导致 label token 仅获 ~0.085% 梯度信号\\\\n- full-sequence loss 下 tail-1024=87.6%, tail-2048=29.6%（灾难性）\\\\n- `--label_only_loss` 修复后 tail-1024=98.24%（+10.6pp），确认 loss dilution 是唯一根因\\\\n- 教训：tokenizer 差异大的模型（特别是 13B+ scale）默认应用 label_only_loss\\\\n- epoch 1 (98.24%) > epoch 3 (97.56%)，轻微过拟合，用 epoch 1 作为报告值\\\\n\\\\n## CodeLlama tail-2048 anomaly (D074, 2026-05-18)\\\\n- v2 (full-sequence): 29.6%, v3 (label_only_loss): 41.8% — 均灾难性失败\\\\n- Tokenizer 分析：CodeLlama 1.21x 膨胀保留 83.3% 内容；DeepSeek 1.32x 膨胀保留 74.4% 却正常 (95%)\\\\n- 结论：tokenization 非唯一原因，更可能是 Llama-2 架构 + LoRA + 长序列稀疏梯度的复合效应\\\\n- 决策：接受为 outlier，论文如实报告，不再投入 GPU 修复\\\\n\\\\n## Universal Irreducible Ceiling (2026-05-16)\\\\n- 所有 4 个模型（Qwen-7B/1.5B, CodeLlama-13B, DeepSeek-6.7B）在 exception_only 条件下共享完全相同的 2 个错误：index 901, 905\\\\n- 均来自 HumanEval/95 (check_dict_case)：`all()` 短路 generator 产生 GeneratorExit，显示为 [ERROR] 但实际 pass\\\\n- 根因：exception_only 表示的语义歧义——不是模型能力问题，是表示层面的硬上限\\\\n- DeepSeek 额外 6 错来自 MBPP/113 (caught ValueError)，其他 3 模型能正确判断\\\\n- 论文价值：证明 2/1190 ceiling 是数据标注 artifact，非模型限制\\\\n\\\\n## Error Analysis 核心发现 (2026-05-18)\\\\n- Exception-based 条件（ae_only/exception_only/vota）错误 100% FN，零 FP — CI/CD 安全性\\\\n- full_trace 独有 58 错中 76% 是 FP — 更多 trace 信息引入噪声\\\\n- exception_only 2 个错误与其他条件零重叠（独有 failure mode）\\\\n- MBPP/284, 419, 443 顽固问题：no_trace/loop/full_trace 全错，exception_only 全对\\\\n- 论文价值：information selectivity 叙事\\\\n\\\\n## Format Marker 贡献量 (2026-05-16)\\\\n- nomarker_seed42 (Qwen-7B): 96.55%，对比 exception_only_seed42 99.83% → marker 贡献 ~3.3pp\\\\n- nomarker_seed42 (Qwen-1.5B): 97.8%，证明 1.5B 无 marker 仍超 7B 无 marker\\\\n- 待 seed123/456 完成后取均值 ± std\\\\n\\\\n## DeepSeek seed123 = 91.09% 异常 (已诊断)\\\\n- 根因：LoRA 初始化失败导致 truncation boundary 处 format collapse\\\\n- 不是信号不足问题，是随机种子 + 架构交互的 edge case\\\\n\\\\n## Zero-Shot Baseline 设计决策 (2026-05-17)\\\\n- Qwen 用 generation-based（模型输出 yes/no，映射为 pass/fail）\\\\n- DeepSeek 用 logit-based classification（比较 yes/pass vs no/fail token logit），因为 DeepSeek chat template 生成解释性文本而非单词\\\\n- 两种方法都是公平的 zero-shot 评估，但方法差异可能部分解释 accuracy 差异（Qwen 89.08% vs DeepSeek 90.59%）\\\\n- 关键 insight：zero-shot 错误模式互补——Qwen 漏判 fail（fail recall 67%），DeepSeek 误判 pass 为 fail（pass recall 89%）\\\\n- Regex baseline (93.61%) > zero-shot LLM (~90%)：说明 exception trace 判别不是通用语言理解任务，pattern matching 反而更有效；SFT 提供 task-specific signal 才能突破\\\\n\\\", \\\"total_chars\\\": 3075, \\\"truncated\\\": false}, \\\"error\\\": null, \\\"metadata\\\": {}}\"}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01McXxEXLRy8VsxNrVngzQWq","session_id":"7ac75d31-96a3-4e39-809a-0eaa527fab20","uuid":"e3d288d0-932e-49df-867d-14ff2453a6f7","timestamp":"2026-05-18T12:31:34.962Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_016Kgh8KJjFA85f8zLLyuQ35","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_014GiXhmcw8VgX2bLmLKLmok","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","prompt":"You are Reviewer R2 for a top ML venue (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 6=Strong Accept). Your focus is **novelty, positioning, and related work**.\n\n## Paper Summary\n**Title:** \"Exception Events as Diagnostic Signals for Verdict Prediction: A Component Ablation Study Across Four Code LLMs\"\n\nThe paper studies verdict prediction (binary pass/fail classification of test executions) and asks: what is the minimal sufficient diagnostic signal within execution traces? Using VOTA (Verdict-Oriented Trace Abstraction), traces are decomposed into exception summaries, loop abstractions, and assertion-point comparisons. Through ablation across 4 models (Qwen-2.5-Coder-7B, DeepSeek-Coder-6.7B, Qwen-1.5B, CodeLlama-13B), the central finding is that exception event summaries alone achieve the highest accuracy (99.83% on Qwen-7B, ≥99.3% on 3/4 models in non-outlier seeds).\n\n**Contributions claimed:**\n1. Component ablation across 4 models showing exception events dominate\n2. Information density decomposition: gradient dilution = 26%, information overload = 74% of full_trace-to-VOTA gap\n3. Methodological findings on LoRA initialization sensitivity (training loss masks 8.2pp accuracy gap)\n\n## Paper Structure\n\n**Introduction (Section 1):**\n- Motivates from DCSH (Ni et al., 2024) and TracePile (Ding et al., 2025) showing inconsistent benefits of trace-augmented SFT\n- Positions verdict prediction as binary classification (fault detection) distinct from code generation\n- Claims no prior work has systematically investigated which trace components matter for verdict tasks\n- Three contributions: component ablation, information density decomposition, LoRA training stability\n\n**Related Work (Section 2):**\n- Three subsections: Trace-augmented code LLMs, Test verdict prediction, LoRA training stability\n- Cites: DCSH (Ni et al., 2024), TracePile (Ding et al., 2025), TRACED (Bouzenia et al., 2024), Lam et al. 2019, Parry et al. 2022, LoRA (Hu et al., 2022), QLoRA (Dettmers et al., 2023)\n- Total references: ~9 papers\n\n**Method (Section 3):**\n- VOTA pipeline: exception extraction → loop abstraction → assertion-point extraction\n- Dataset: HumanEval + MBPP-sanitized, 591 problems, 5,910 samples\n\n**Experiments (Section 4):**\n- 4.1: Component ablation on Qwen-7B (Table 1)\n- 4.2: Cross-model (Qwen + DeepSeek, Table 2)\n- 4.3: 4-model exception ceiling (Table 3)\n- 4.4: Seed robustness\n- 4.5: Info density analysis\n- 4.6: SFT vs few-shot (Table 4) — gap is only 2.7pp to best few-shot\n- 4.7: OOD generalization (Table 5) — 19.1pp degradation cross-language\n- 4.8: Error analysis (Table 6) — no novel findings\n- 4.9: LoRA init sensitivity (Table 7)\n- 4.10: Cross-seed token budget (Table 8)\n\n**Discussion (Section 5), Limitations (Section 6), Conclusion (Section 7)**\n**Appendix A:** Token budget curve (trivially monotonic), **Appendix B:** Training dynamics (trivial)\n\n**Key Data Points:**\n- exception_only: 99.83% (Qwen-7B), 99.33% (DeepSeek, median of seeds), 99.83% (Qwen-1.5B, CodeLlama-13B)\n- DeepSeek seed 123 outlier: 91.09% with train loss 0.1220 (vs 0.1225 for seed 456 at 99.33%)\n- SFT advantage over best few-shot: only 2.7pp\n- OOD degradation: -19.1pp mean across Java/JS/C++\n- Budget curve: monotonically increasing, no phase transition\n\n## Your Task\nProvide a detailed review focusing on:\n\n1. **Novelty Assessment**: Is the core finding (exceptions are the key signal) surprising or obvious? Does it advance understanding?\n2. **Related Work Coverage**: Are key related works cited? Is the positioning fair?\n3. **Contribution Significance**: Are the contributions sufficient for a top venue? Is each contribution genuinely new?\n4. **Framing and Narrative**: Is the story compelling? Does the paper oversell or undersell?\n5. **Broader Impact**: Does this paper change how practitioners or researchers think about trace-based training?\n6. **Writing Quality**: Is the paper well-written? Any AI writing artifacts?\n\nFormat your review as:\n```\n## R2 Review: Novelty and Related Work\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentences]\n\n### Strengths\n[Numbered list]\n\n### Weaknesses\n[Numbered list, with severity: Major/Minor]\n\n### Questions for Authors\n[Numbered list]\n\n### Novelty Assessment\n[Detailed paragraph]\n\n### Related Work Gaps\n[If any]\n\n### Detailed Comments\n[Section-by-section if needed]\n\n### Preliminary Score: X/6\n### Confidence: X/5\n```"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":15607,"cache_read_input_tokens":35128,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":15607},"output_tokens":17,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7ac75d31-96a3-4e39-809a-0eaa527fab20","uuid":"0a65ad69-ab72-45d2-8343-0cf062e03e03"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"adc5b94a3dd57f6ae","tool_use_id":"toolu_014GiXhmcw8VgX2bLmLKLmok","description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer R2 for a top ML venue (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 6=Strong Accept). Your focus is **novelty, positioning, and related work**.\n\n## Paper Summary\n**Title:** \"Exception Events as Diagnostic Signals for Verdict Prediction: A Component Ablation Study Across Four Code LLMs\"\n\nThe paper studies verdict prediction (binary pass/fail classification of test executions) and asks: what is the minimal sufficient diagnostic signal within execution traces? Using VOTA (Verdict-Oriented Trace Abstraction), traces are decomposed into exception summaries, loop abstractions, and assertion-point comparisons. Through ablation across 4 models (Qwen-2.5-Coder-7B, DeepSeek-Coder-6.7B, Qwen-1.5B, CodeLlama-13B), the central finding is that exception event summaries alone achieve the highest accuracy (99.83% on Qwen-7B, ≥99.3% on 3/4 models in non-outlier seeds).\n\n**Contributions claimed:**\n1. Component ablation across 4 models showing exception events dominate\n2. Information density decomposition: gradient dilution = 26%, information overload = 74% of full_trace-to-VOTA gap\n3. Methodological findings on LoRA initialization sensitivity (training loss masks 8.2pp accuracy gap)\n\n## Paper Structure\n\n**Introduction (Section 1):**\n- Motivates from DCSH (Ni et al., 2024) and TracePile (Ding et al., 2025) showing inconsistent benefits of trace-augmented SFT\n- Positions verdict prediction as binary classification (fault detection) distinct from code generation\n- Claims no prior work has systematically investigated which trace components matter for verdict tasks\n- Three contributions: component ablation, information density decomposition, LoRA training stability\n\n**Related Work (Section 2):**\n- Three subsections: Trace-augmented code LLMs, Test verdict prediction, LoRA training stability\n- Cites: DCSH (Ni et al., 2024), TracePile (Ding et al., 2025), TRACED (Bouzenia et al., 2024), Lam et al. 2019, Parry et al. 2022, LoRA (Hu et al., 2022), QLoRA (Dettmers et al., 2023)\n- Total references: ~9 papers\n\n**Method (Section 3):**\n- VOTA pipeline: exception extraction → loop abstraction → assertion-point extraction\n- Dataset: HumanEval + MBPP-sanitized, 591 problems, 5,910 samples\n\n**Experiments (Section 4):**\n- 4.1: Component ablation on Qwen-7B (Table 1)\n- 4.2: Cross-model (Qwen + DeepSeek, Table 2)\n- 4.3: 4-model exception ceiling (Table 3)\n- 4.4: Seed robustness\n- 4.5: Info density analysis\n- 4.6: SFT vs few-shot (Table 4) — gap is only 2.7pp to best few-shot\n- 4.7: OOD generalization (Table 5) — 19.1pp degradation cross-language\n- 4.8: Error analysis (Table 6) — no novel findings\n- 4.9: LoRA init sensitivity (Table 7)\n- 4.10: Cross-seed token budget (Table 8)\n\n**Discussion (Section 5), Limitations (Section 6), Conclusion (Section 7)**\n**Appendix A:** Token budget curve (trivially monotonic), **Appendix B:** Training dynamics (trivial)\n\n**Key Data Points:**\n- exception_only: 99.83% (Qwen-7B), 99.33% (DeepSeek, median of seeds), 99.83% (Qwen-1.5B, CodeLlama-13B)\n- DeepSeek seed 123 outlier: 91.09% with train loss 0.1220 (vs 0.1225 for seed 456 at 99.33%)\n- SFT advantage over best few-shot: only 2.7pp\n- OOD degradation: -19.1pp mean across Java/JS/C++\n- Budget curve: monotonically increasing, no phase transition\n\n## Your Task\nProvide a detailed review focusing on:\n\n1. **Novelty Assessment**: Is the core finding (exceptions are the key signal) surprising or obvious? Does it advance understanding?\n2. **Related Work Coverage**: Are key related works cited? Is the positioning fair?\n3. **Contribution Significance**: Are the contributions sufficient for a top venue? Is each contribution genuinely new?\n4. **Framing and Narrative**: Is the story compelling? Does the paper oversell or undersell?\n5. **Broader Impact**: Does this paper change how practitioners or researchers think about trace-based training?\n6. **Writing Quality**: Is the paper well-written? Any AI writing artifacts?\n\nFormat your review as:\n```\n## R2 Review: Novelty and Related Work\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentences]\n\n### Strengths\n[Numbered list]\n\n### Weaknesses\n[Numbered list, with severity: Major/Minor]\n\n### Questions for Authors\n[Numbered list]\n\n### Novelty Assessment\n[Detailed paragraph]\n\n### Related Work Gaps\n[If any]\n\n### Detailed Comments\n[Section-by-section if needed]\n\n### Preliminary Score: X/6\n### Confidence: X/5\n```","uuid":"2a3b3a15-0952-4460-acf2-7861c3b2d12a","session_id":"7ac75d31-96a3-4e39-809a-0eaa527fab20"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are Reviewer R2 for a top ML venue (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 6=Strong Accept). Your focus is **novelty, positioning, and related work**.\n\n## Paper Summary\n**Title:** \"Exception Events as Diagnostic Signals for Verdict Prediction: A Component Ablation Study Across Four Code LLMs\"\n\nThe paper studies verdict prediction (binary pass/fail classification of test executions) and asks: what is the minimal sufficient diagnostic signal within execution traces? Using VOTA (Verdict-Oriented Trace Abstraction), traces are decomposed into exception summaries, loop abstractions, and assertion-point comparisons. Through ablation across 4 models (Qwen-2.5-Coder-7B, DeepSeek-Coder-6.7B, Qwen-1.5B, CodeLlama-13B), the central finding is that exception event summaries alone achieve the highest accuracy (99.83% on Qwen-7B, ≥99.3% on 3/4 models in non-outlier seeds).\n\n**Contributions claimed:**\n1. Component ablation across 4 models showing exception events dominate\n2. Information density decomposition: gradient dilution = 26%, information overload = 74% of full_trace-to-VOTA gap\n3. Methodological findings on LoRA initialization sensitivity (training loss masks 8.2pp accuracy gap)\n\n## Paper Structure\n\n**Introduction (Section 1):**\n- Motivates from DCSH (Ni et al., 2024) and TracePile (Ding et al., 2025) showing inconsistent benefits of trace-augmented SFT\n- Positions verdict prediction as binary classification (fault detection) distinct from code generation\n- Claims no prior work has systematically investigated which trace components matter for verdict tasks\n- Three contributions: component ablation, information density decomposition, LoRA training stability\n\n**Related Work (Section 2):**\n- Three subsections: Trace-augmented code LLMs, Test verdict prediction, LoRA training stability\n- Cites: DCSH (Ni et al., 2024), TracePile (Ding et al., 2025), TRACED (Bouzenia et al., 2024), Lam et al. 2019, Parry et al. 2022, LoRA (Hu et al., 2022), QLoRA (Dettmers et al., 2023)\n- Total references: ~9 papers\n\n**Method (Section 3):**\n- VOTA pipeline: exception extraction → loop abstraction → assertion-point extraction\n- Dataset: HumanEval + MBPP-sanitized, 591 problems, 5,910 samples\n\n**Experiments (Section 4):**\n- 4.1: Component ablation on Qwen-7B (Table 1)\n- 4.2: Cross-model (Qwen + DeepSeek, Table 2)\n- 4.3: 4-model exception ceiling (Table 3)\n- 4.4: Seed robustness\n- 4.5: Info density analysis\n- 4.6: SFT vs few-shot (Table 4) — gap is only 2.7pp to best few-shot\n- 4.7: OOD generalization (Table 5) — 19.1pp degradation cross-language\n- 4.8: Error analysis (Table 6) — no novel findings\n- 4.9: LoRA init sensitivity (Table 7)\n- 4.10: Cross-seed token budget (Table 8)\n\n**Discussion (Section 5), Limitations (Section 6), Conclusion (Section 7)**\n**Appendix A:** Token budget curve (trivially monotonic), **Appendix B:** Training dynamics (trivial)\n\n**Key Data Points:**\n- exception_only: 99.83% (Qwen-7B), 99.33% (DeepSeek, median of seeds), 99.83% (Qwen-1.5B, CodeLlama-13B)\n- DeepSeek seed 123 outlier: 91.09% with train loss 0.1220 (vs 0.1225 for seed 456 at 99.33%)\n- SFT advantage over best few-shot: only 2.7pp\n- OOD degradation: -19.1pp mean across Java/JS/C++\n- Budget curve: monotonically increasing, no phase transition\n\n## Your Task\nProvide a detailed review focusing on:\n\n1. **Novelty Assessment**: Is the core finding (exceptions are the key signal) surprising or obvious? Does it advance understanding?\n2. **Related Work Coverage**: Are key related works cited? Is the positioning fair?\n3. **Contribution Significance**: Are the contributions sufficient for a top venue? Is each contribution genuinely new?\n4. **Framing and Narrative**: Is the story compelling? Does the paper oversell or undersell?\n5. **Broader Impact**: Does this paper change how practitioners or researchers think about trace-based training?\n6. **Writing Quality**: Is the paper well-written? Any AI writing artifacts?\n\nFormat your review as:\n```\n## R2 Review: Novelty and Related Work\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentences]\n\n### Strengths\n[Numbered list]\n\n### Weaknesses\n[Numbered list, with severity: Major/Minor]\n\n### Questions for Authors\n[Numbered list]\n\n### Novelty Assessment\n[Detailed paragraph]\n\n### Related Work Gaps\n[If any]\n\n### Detailed Comments\n[Section-by-section if needed]\n\n### Preliminary Score: X/6\n### Confidence: X/5\n```"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_014GiXhmcw8VgX2bLmLKLmok","session_id":"7ac75d31-96a3-4e39-809a-0eaa527fab20","uuid":"597390d5-057a-456b-8199-db28bed272b8","timestamp":"2026-05-18T12:31:38.359Z"}
